    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d16">

                <pb facs="0032" n="22" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg22"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European Advisory Commission/60:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-01-28T20:00:00-04:00">January 28, 1944—8
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>701. Inasmuch as the paper on French Civil Affairs (your 544, January 20,<anchor
                        n="43" xml:id="d16-f1"/> and 632, January 24) was referred by the Moscow
                    Conference to the European Advisory Commission, our view is that the paper is
                    now formally before the Commission by reference from Moscow, and you are
                    authorized to discuss it on the basis of your present instructions. If the
                    Russians have any important objections, they will no doubt be developed in the
                    course of the discussions, and we can consider them upon report from you.</p>
                <p>Should there be any fundamental change here, such as envisaged in your 632 of
                    January 24, you will be promptly informed.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
